// SSU instructional materials 2025-2026: evidence summaries, goals 1, 4 and 5.
// These render publicly under Evidence Summary in the campus report.

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-1.1-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State has a published campus policy governing the adoption of course instructional materials. The Course Instructional Materials Adoption policy, recommended by the Academic Senate, remains in force. It requires departments to set adoption deadlines. It obliges chairs to communicate those deadlines, including to faculty hired after the ordering window closes. The bookstore operates the Barnes and Noble adoption platform, where faculty submit their orders. The platform shows which courses have no order. Steve Higginbotham, the bookstore manager, has reported adoption data to campus representatives for close to a decade. For fall 2026 the campus recorded 1,237 adoptions submitted and 220 outstanding. The campus reaches Established because the process is set out in a published policy and a named office holds responsibility for it. It does not reach Managed because no measure of success is defined for adoption timeliness. The policy's scheduled review date passed in December 2015.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-1.2-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State operates a textbook adoption compliance effort and measures the result each term. The bookstore has run the Barnes and Noble adoption platform since fall 2020. The platform shows which courses have no order. Bookstore staff time is committed to weekly communications to faculty during and after the ordering window. Spreadsheets of courses without orders go to deans and department chairs. Chairs raise the deadline in faculty meetings. The bookstore, the Center for Teaching and Educational Technology and the ATI committee produced a video series on the adoption deadline. New faculty hear the deadline during orientation. Submission rates moved from 54 percent in spring 2024 to 65 percent in fall 2024, with a further 16 percent gain across 2024-2025. The campus reaches Established because the four elements the indicator names are all in place: documented practices, committed staff time, training resources, and the technology to track adoption. It does not reach Managed because no target or milestone is defined against which those percentages could be judged.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-1.6-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State's bookstore reports textbook adoption performance to campus administration on a predictable cycle. Steve Higginbotham reports to campus representatives and the ATI committee every other month, in advance of the ordering cycle. Adoption spreadsheets reach deans, who pass them to faculty in their schools. Each report states the number of orders submitted and the number outstanding, which for fall 2026 stood at 1,237 and 220. The bookstore manager holds responsibility for producing and distributing them, and has done so for close to a decade. The campus reaches Defined because the reporting runs on schedule and reaches people who can act on it. It does not reach Established because no semester's report is stored where the campus can retrieve it. The most recent figures were given verbally with no export behind them. The reports do not address whether adopted materials will be ready in accessible formats.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-4.1-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State requires course syllabi to be delivered through the learning management system in an accessible format. The Syllabus policy states that syllabi shall be provided in a format accessible to all students with the content built into the university learning management system. The Center for Teaching and Educational Technology publishes the SSU Canvas Course Design Template through Canvas Commons. Accessibility is covered at new faculty orientation, in the Canvas professional development levels, and in workshops offered each semester. UDOIT runs across Canvas courses and shows faculty where their own content falls short. The campus reaches Defined because the expectation is written into policy and the supports behind it exist. It does not reach Established because nothing shows that course materials reach the learning management system consistently across academic units. No measure tracks it. The Syllabus policy's scheduled review date passed in March 2023.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-4.3-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State publishes accessibility guidelines for Canvas course content. The campus accessibility site holds a faculty accessibility guide, with separate guidance on documents and multimedia. The Center for Teaching and Educational Technology maintains its own accessibility pages. The Accessible Syllabus Policy requires syllabi in an accessible format and directs faculty to the accessible template. The SSU Canvas Course Design Template applies universal design principles to course structure and is available through Canvas Commons. The Accessible Content Partnership remediates Canvas courses on a term schedule unless the instructor declines through their dean. The campus reaches Established because the guidelines are published and current. It does not reach Managed because no measure records whether the guidance changes what faculty produce.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-4.5-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State gives alternate media staff standing access to course content through a defined account role. Disability Services for Students holds an account role reaching Canvas course content directly, which removes the delay of asking each instructor for materials. The Accessible Content Partnership enrols courses by default rather than on request, so remediation can begin earlier in the term. Late textbook orders delay alternate format production for about two thousand students a semester. The campus reaches Defined because the access arrangement operates every term and the staff who depend on it describe it consistently. It does not reach Established because no document records the roles, permissions or timelines. The indicator also covers platforms beyond the learning management system, where faculty adopt publisher tools that alternate media staff cannot reach. A concern recorded in September 2026 states that alternate media production now depends on student assistants following staff departures, with no continuity of training.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-4.11-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State reviews the accessibility of software it purchases, and has no process addressing websites and web applications that faculty maintain themselves. Information Technology and Procurement review faculty software licence requests, including publisher tools that connect to Canvas, for accessibility conformance before approval. The Center for Teaching and Educational Technology remediates Canvas courses on an opt-out basis, which covers content held inside the learning management system. Neither activity reaches a site a faculty member builds and maintains, whether on the campus domain or elsewhere. No method exists for finding such sites. The campus reaches Initiated because related review activity exists and touches some faculty-facing tools. It does not reach Defined because the indicator's own subject has no identified owner, no review method, and no record of any review having taken place.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-5.11-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State captions instructional audio and video through a campus platform and corrects those captions in house. Captioning moved to Panopto after the previous platform's licence ended. The Center for Teaching and Educational Technology corrects automatically generated captions as part of its own work. Two published policies govern how courses and their materials are selected and delivered: Course Instructional Materials Adoption, and Course Modality. The campus reaches Defined because a captioning pipeline operates for instructional media. It does not reach Established because the caption correction work has no written procedure. John Lynch raised whether automatically generated captions meet accessibility standards, and the campus has not answered that question.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-5.12-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State remediates course readers when a student accommodation requires it. Accessibility is addressed at no earlier point in how readers are produced. Disability Services for Students converts print and digital course materials into accessible formats on request, with four published pages describing the service. The bookstore works with Disability Services on the formats in which reader content is supplied. An external publishing partner handles copyright clearance and indemnification for course readers, which is a separate matter from accessibility. The campus reaches Defined because a reader production pipeline operates and Disability Services takes part in it. It does not reach Established because nothing addresses accessibility when a reader is created, selected or adopted. The only accessibility step on record begins after a student has registered an accommodation.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-5.13-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State operates a remediation pipeline for course documents, run by a named specialist whose position description covers the work. The Center for Teaching and Educational Technology remediates PDFs using Equidox and Adobe Acrobat, with a student team trained on both. The Canvas Remediation Manual sets out the path that team follows. The Accessible Syllabus Policy requires accessible syllabi. The accessible syllabus template gives faculty a starting document. The SSU Canvas Course Design Template applies universal design principles to course structure. Tim Hensel holds the Accessibility Specialist role, and accessible instructional materials responsibilities were added to his position description in September 2026. The campus reaches Established because the procedures are consistent and responsibility is documented in a position description rather than absorbed informally. It does not reach Managed because no measure records how many documents are remediated or to what standard. The remediation manual sits inside a Canvas course that cannot be opened from outside the centre.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-5.14-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State reviews emerging instructional technology for accessibility through the regional purchase review, and holds no campus process of its own for adopting such tools. Instructional technology purchases route through the SFBRN IT Accessibility Review Procedure. That procedure evaluates a product's accessibility conformance against WCAG 2.1 A and AA. It rates risk by how many people must use the product and whether use is required for coursework. It requires a signed alternative access plan before a high-risk product is approved. The Academic Technology and Instructional Space Subcommittee considers major educational technology, and an accessibility analyst supports the purchase review alongside Procurement. The campus reaches Defined because tools receive accessibility review before instructional use and the route they take is written down. It does not reach Established because the regional procurement process operates that review rather than Sonoma. No Sonoma practice records what follows approval, including how alternate activities are arranged when a tool cannot be made fully accessible.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-5.15-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State evaluates publisher tools for accessibility when they are purchased. Information Technology and Procurement review faculty requests for publisher platforms that connect to Canvas before those tools are approved. Disability Services for Students supplies accessible alternatives when a student accommodation requires one. The campus reaches Defined because publisher material meets an accessibility check at the point of purchase and a remediation route sits behind it. It does not reach Established because no record holds publisher accessibility conformance reports for instructional content. Nothing is planned in advance of a student needing an alternative.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-5.16-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State routes learning and engagement tools through accessibility review before instructional use. Tools that carry no purchase cost are submitted as zero dollar requisitions, which sends them through the same accessibility and security review as a paid purchase. Amanda McGowan described that route in October 2026. The review is the SFBRN IT Accessibility Review Procedure, a written procedure with its own risk rules and communication templates. The Academic Technology and Instructional Space Subcommittee reviews major educational technology for the campus. The campus reaches Defined because tools reach a review and the reviewing procedure exists in writing. It does not reach Established because no Sonoma procedure records the zero dollar route. The regional process operates the review rather than the campus.";
